{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Nullable Value Types.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Nullable Value Types.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"nullable value types nullable value types","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Nullable Value Types.md","fileName":"Nullable Value Types.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Nullable Value Types","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-08 21:16\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[C Sharp Language and Type Fundamentals]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["C Sharp Language and Type Fundamentals","c80andnetcore30moderncross-platformdevelopment.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Nullable Value Types.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Nullable Value Types.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"nullable value types nullable value types","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Nullable Value Types.md","fileName":"Nullable Value Types.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Nullable Value Types","heading":"Nullable Value Types","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A value type normally always contains a value, but appending `?` creates a nullable form that can also represent `null`. This is useful when a number, date, or other value is absent in an external source such as a database.\n\nA nullable value reports whether it has a value and exposes that value only when present. Code should test or safely unwrap it before use. The null-coalescing operator can provide a fallback when absence has a sensible default.","tags":["baby"],"links":["C Sharp Language and Type Fundamentals","c80andnetcore30moderncross-platformdevelopment.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Nullable Value Types.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Nullable Value Types.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourc